# Copy to .deploy.env (chmod 600) and fill in. Sourced by scripts/deploy.sh.
# .deploy.env is gitignored — never commit the real webhook.

# Discord webhook. Serves TWO purposes on a PM2 host, and that is deliberate:
#   1. deploy notifications from this script (success / fail / fleet-OTA);
#   2. it is exported into the environment `pm2 reload --update-env` pushes
#      into duckdb-service and image-service, where the ADR-005 silence
#      watcher needs it to alert on a module going quiet.
# Leave unset and BOTH go quiet: deploy notifications log locally to
# logs/auto-deploy.log only (the deploy still runs), and the silence watcher
# degrades to a print() nobody reads.
DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL=

# --- Environment pushed into the pm2 apps on every reload -------------------
# scripts/deploy.sh sources this file with `set -a` and then runs
# `pm2 reload <app> --update-env`, which refreshes each app's environment from
# THIS shell. So these are not merely deploy-script settings: whatever is here
# wins over ecosystem.config.js on the next automatic tick. Keep them in sync
# with the ecosystem file (see production-runbook.md) or a deploy will quietly
# strip them.

# SET THESE TWO TOGETHER OR NEITHER.
#
# HIGHFIVE_ENV=production makes services/prod_guard.py refuse to boot on the
# public dev-key fallback (2026-07 audit, #204) — without it the guard is a
# no-op, which is why it must be explicit. But the guard also refuses to boot
# on an EMPTY key, so exporting HIGHFIVE_ENV=production with HIGHFIVE_API_KEY
# blank crash-loops duckdb-service and image-service under pm2 autorestart,
# with the reason visible only in `pm2 logs`.
#
# Both lines are therefore left commented out. Uncomment them together, with a
# real key, once you have one:
#
#   HIGHFIVE_ENV=production
#   HIGHFIVE_API_KEY=<same value as /var/www/highfive/.env>
#
# (The key must match the backend's — the #171 admin server-logs proxy
# authenticates against the Flask services' internal /logs gate with it.)

# Autonomous, test-gated firmware OTA. When 1, a firmware-source change on main
# (or an in-tree SEQUENCE ahead of what's served) triggers: native test gate
# (pio test -e native) -> build.sh -> publish OTA -> bump/commit/push/tag.
# Forward-only: once a good manifest publishes the fleet pulls it and there is
# NO field rollback. Set 0 to deploy services only and never touch firmware.
FIRMWARE_AUTO_OTA=1

# Optional. If unset, ESP32-CAM/build.sh reads the gitignored ESP32-CAM/GEO_API_KEY
# file (already present on the prod host).
# GEO_API_KEY=
